Q: Getting a folder ID in as few SQL statements as possible A: <p>One solution I just came up is to use a series of LEFT JOINs on the same table.</p>

<pre><code>SELECT tb3.id FROM folders AS tb1
LEFT JOIN folders AS tb2 ON tb2.parent_id=tb1.id
LEFT JOIN folders AS tb3 ON tb3.parent_id=tb2.id
WHERE tb1.name='a' AND tb2.name='b' AND tb3.name='c'
LIMIT 1
</code></pre>

<p>Based on the number of folders in the path, I can dynamically generate the above SQL.
I'm not sure if this is the perfect solution, but right now it's working as intended.</p>

<p>Any feedback on whether this is a good idea or not is greatly appreciated!</p>

<pre><code>$path = 'a/b/c';
$path = explode('/', $path);

$sql = "SELECT tb".count($path).".id FROM folders AS tb1";
for($x=1, $max = count($path); $x&lt;=$max; $x++) {
    $sql .= " LEFT JOIN folders AS tb".($i+2);
    $sql .= " ON tb".($i+2).".parent_id = tb"($i+1).".id";
}

$sql .= " WHERE 1";

foreach($path AS $x =&gt; $p) {
    $sql .= " AND tb".($x=1).".name='".$p."'";
}

$sql .= " LIMIT 1";
</code></pre>

Q: Converting a PHP object array to a JavaScript object array A: <p>Your string already contains valid Javascript/JSON. You don't need to parse it again. Let's simplify it a bit and look at where it might go wrong:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>echo $convertData; // [{&quot;services_idno&quot;:1,&quot;services_name&quot;:&quot;Front-End Web Development&quot;}]
</code></pre>
<p>Now, let's look at your script tag:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
let portfolioData = JSON.parse(&quot;&lt;?php echo $convertData;?&gt;&quot;);
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>When rendered, this will result in the following content being sent to the browser:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
let portfolioData = JSON.parse(&quot;[{&quot;services_idno&quot;:1,&quot;services_name&quot;:&quot;Front-End Web Development&quot;}]&quot;);
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Do you see the problem?</p>
<p>So you have two options:</p>
<ol>
<li>Properly escape/encode your string to a <em>JSON string</em> and parse it as JSON</li>
<li>Don't parse it, since it already is valid JSON</li>
</ol>
<p>I suggest option 2:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
let portfolioData = &lt;?php echo $convertData;?&gt;;
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Rendered output:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
let portfolioData = [{&quot;services_idno&quot;:1,&quot;services_name&quot;:&quot;Front-End Web Development&quot;}];
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How to implement dynamic query identifiers based on a dynamic table name A: <p>You can get the id name in this way:</p>

<pre><code>$id_name = preg_replace('/.*_(.*)/', '${1}_id', $db_table);
</code></pre>

<p>then</p>

<pre><code>$this-&gt;db-&gt;select($id_name . ',name');
</code></pre>

Q: Android NDK: Why is AAssetManager_open returning NULL A: <p>In my case, I first called</p>
<pre><code>AAssetManager * mgr = AAssetManager_fromJava(env, global_asset_manager);
AAssetDir* assetDir = AAssetManager_openDir(mgr, &quot;font&quot;);
const char * filename = AAssetDir_getNextFileName(assetDir)
</code></pre>
<p>, where <code>global_asset_manager</code> was constructed as described in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/22436260/3474552">this answer</a> and <code>&quot;font&quot;</code> is a directory containing the asset that I wanted to read.  These calls were successful and returned a <code>filename</code> that I recognized.  I then assumed the working directory of <code>AAssetManager * mgr</code> was <code>font</code> at that point, so that I could call</p>
<pre><code>AAsset* asset = AAssetManager_open(mgr, filename, AASSET_MODE_STREAMING);
</code></pre>
<p>and have the file path in <code>filename</code> properly resolved.  But, this returned <code>NULL</code>.  It turns out that the directory information needs to be re-specified, so what ended up working was</p>
<pre><code>
char* full_filepath = new char[100];
strcpy(full_filepath, &quot;font/&quot;);
strcat(full_filepath, filename);
AAsset* asset = AAssetManager_open(mgr, full_filepath, AASSET_MODE_STREAMING);
</code></pre>
